{
  "teamId": 144,
  "year": 2026,
  "narrative": "The 2026 Atlanta Braves arrived with something to prove, and early on they made their case loudly. A blistering April — 19 wins against just 8 losses — set the tone, powered by Matt Olson's booming home runs and a lineup that could erupt at any moment. Dominic Smith's two-run double in mid-April and Olson's ninth home run to close the month announced that this was a team with genuine offensive teeth, backed by a rotation and bullpen capable of posting a 3.63 ERA over a full season.\n\nMay and early June brought the first tests of character. The offense kept producing — Michael Harris II was emerging as a star, and Chadwick Tromp delivered in crucial spots — but the pitching began to show cracks. Bryce Elder and Dylan Lee were torched in high-leverage moments, and the bullpen, particularly Raisel Iglesias, became a source of real anxiety. June cratered to 9-15, the one true stumble of the season, as Iglesias surrendered a gut-punch two-run homer that encapsulated the month's misery. Yet even in the rubble, Ozzie Albies answered with a massive two-run blast on June 20 that hinted the Braves were not broken, merely tested.\n\nJuly and August told the story of a team rediscovering itself. The record swung back to 16-11 and then 17-11 as the lineup deepened with contributions from Joey Bart, Mauricio Dubón capitalizing on a Masyn Winn error, and the mid-season addition of Ha-Seong Kim, who slotted in smoothly and delivered in the clutch. Michael Harris II's August double was a thing of beauty, and Sean Murphy's September grand slam and walk-off single in an epic September 11 rally — capped by Drake Baldwin's go-ahead double — crystallized a team that had grown into something formidable. Ronald Acuña Jr.'s grand slam on September 19 put an exclamation point on a 94-win campaign.\n\nThe postseason began with promise and ended in heartbreak. Atlanta dispatched their Wild Card opponent behind Austin Riley's three-run blast and solid contributions across the roster, advancing to the NLDS with momentum. But the Division Series proved a bridge too far. Robert Suarez surrendered the decisive blow as the Dodgers surged late, sending the Braves home with 94 wins and a division series exit that will sting through the winter. A team that survived a brutal June collapse and won with depth, pitching, and timely hitting ultimately ran into a buzzsaw in October, leaving unfinished business hanging over Truist Park.",
